<h2>The case</h2>
<p>The petitioner, a member of a paramilitary force, apprehended arrest in Uluberia Police Station Case No. 641 of 2026 dated 19 August 2026 (G.R. Case No. 2843 of 2026), registered under Section 69 (sexual intercourse on false promise of marriage), Section 318(4) (cheating) and Section 351(3) (criminal intimidation) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. He moved the Calcutta High Court for anticipatory bail — the application being treated under the provisions corresponding to Section 438 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, now Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023.</p>
<p>What distinguished the matter was the complaint itself: the FIR disclosed that the relationship between the complainant and the petitioner had been running since 2018. The allegation, in substance, was of sexual exploitation on a promise of marriage; the defence pointed to the length and continuity of the association as inconsistent with deceit at the inception.</p>

<h2>What the court held</h2>
<p>Justice Tirthankar Ghosh granted the protection, recording that &ldquo;custodial detention of the present petitioner in connection with the instant case is unwarranted&rdquo;. The operative directions balanced liberty with the needs of the investigation:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Time-limited protection.</strong> The anticipatory bail order was to remain in force for six weeks — a window within which the petitioner must regularise his position before the appropriate court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Weekly attendance.</strong> The petitioner was directed to meet the investigating officer once every week, keeping the investigation supplied with his presence without the need for custody.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Statutory conditions.</strong> The conditions of Section 438(2) CrPC — now Section 482(2) BNSS — were imposed: availability for interrogation, no inducement or threat to persons acquainted with the facts, and no leaving India without leave.</div>
</div>

<h2>Section 69 BNS at the pre-arrest stage</h2>
<p>Section 69 criminalises a promise of marriage made <em>without any intention of fulfilling it</em>, employed as the means of obtaining consent. Intention at the inception is the fulcrum. At the anticipatory bail stage, courts do not conduct a trial of that intention; they ask whether the FIR&rsquo;s own narrative, taken as it stands, points to deceit from the start or to a relationship that soured. The longer and more layered the association disclosed — years of cohabitation or courtship, family-level acknowledgement, continued relations after alleged refusals — the harder it is to treat the inception as fraudulent for the purpose of justifying arrest before trial.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor on the face of the FIR</th><th>Bearing at the anticipatory bail stage</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Relationship of long duration disclosed in the complaint itself</td><td>Weakens the inference of a false promise at inception; supports pre-arrest protection.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Allegation of a promise made and broken shortly before the complaint</td><td>Points towards breach rather than deceit — a civil wrong is not Section 69.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Specific material suggesting the accused never intended to marry (concealed marriage, parallel engagements)</td><td>Strengthens the prosecution case and the plea for custodial interrogation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Accompanying charges — cheating under Section 318(4), intimidation under Section 351(3) BNS</td><td>Examined on their own ingredients; they do not automatically harden the Section 69 allegation.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>The complaint&rsquo;s own timeline is the first battleground: a relationship the FIR traces back years is a structural fact the prosecution cannot easily argue around at the threshold.</p>
  <p>Anticipatory bail in such cases is increasingly granted on terms — limited duration, periodic attendance before the investigating officer — rather than as open-ended immunity. The accused must use the window to seek regular bail or further protection.</p>
  <p>Members of disciplined forces face service consequences from arrest itself, which makes the pre-arrest remedy especially consequential; courts, in turn, weigh their traceability and institutional accountability in their favour.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article is an informational note on a reported order; it is not legal advice. Pre-arrest bail in Section 69 BNS cases turns closely on the individual record, and outcomes vary with the facts.</div>
